From 9a3d4fdaeee28d1a1c78cc70475ee35fd244e73a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 04:58:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 43/43] codegen_c: pass newtypes and nullable-pointer enums as
 scalars at C ABI boundaries

A `#[repr(transparent)]` (or single-field) struct or an enum using
a non-zero niche (`Option<&T>`, `Option<NonNull<T>>`, ...) is emitted as
a C struct, but Rust passes and returns these like the scalar they wrap.
Where the C ABI treats small structs differently from scalars the two
disagree: 32-bit PowerPC Darwin returns every struct through a hidden
pointer in r3 (verified with Apple GCC 4.2 and GCC 16), and so does i386
SysV, while e.g. `signed char f(void)` returns in r3/%al. Calls to C
functions declared as returning such a type (objc2's `Bool`, Option-of-
pointer returns in bindings, ...) read garbage, and C calling back into
such Rust functions did the reverse.

C ABI functions whose signature contains such a type now use the scalar
instead (other signatures are emitted exactly as before):
- imported functions: the real symbol is declared as `<name>__cabi` with
  the scalar types, and `<name>` is a static inline converting wrapper,
- Rust definitions: `<name>` is the body (direct Rust calls), and the
  exported symbol is a converting `<name>__cabi` wrapper,
- taking the address of either gives `<name>__cabi` (ItemAddr constants,
  fn item to pointer casts, static relocations), C ABI function pointer
  typedefs use the scalar types, and calls through them convert the
  arguments and the return value.

Variadic and naked functions are left as they were.

A Rust-defined function from another crate (no link name, no MIR here)
is declared as both `<name>` and `<name>__cabi`, which that crate
exports itself; binding `<name>__cabi` to the `<name>` symbol would call
the struct-form body with the scalar convention.

